</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution Consistency Calibration.</span>
Although text-guided adversarial fine-tuning already improves over label supervision by leveraging <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math>, directly matching the pre-trained distribution remains suboptimal.
The reason is that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> entangles two factors: the relative image-text similarity structure and the confidence scale inherited from the clean pre-trained model.
The former captures how an image distributes its semantic similarity across all candidate text prompts, beyond the ground-truth label, while the latter is reflected in the magnitudes of the prediction logits.
Directly using <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target in adversarial training therefore forces the robust model to inherit not only the desired semantic relations but also the pre-trained confidence scale, and the latter may be mismatched to the evolving robust feature space, thereby interfering with the preservation of the original cross-modal structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate such structural distortions, we introduce distribution consistency calibration to align the robust model to match the cross-modal semantic structure of the original model. Specifically, it should maintain the relative semantic relationships between the image and multiple texts, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{S_{rob}(i,j)}{\sum_{k}S_{rob}(i,k)}\leftrightarrow\frac{S_{orig}(i,j)}{\sum_{k}S_{orig}(i,k)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{S_{rob}(i,j)}{\sum_{k}S_{rob}(i,k)}\leftrightarrow\frac{S_{orig}(i,j)}{\sum_{k}S_{orig}(i,k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{orig}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{orig}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{rob}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{rob}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> are the similarities between the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th image and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th text in the original pre-trained model and the robust model, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, to maintain distribution consistency, we adjust the confidence scale of the robust model’s predictions by introducing a scaled temperature parameter to the logits of the original pre-trained model. The temperature adjustment reduces overconfident predictions, ensuring that the model focuses on stable semantic features instead of noisy or adversarial perturbations. The resulting temperature-scaled target distribution preserves cross-modal semantic consistency while mitigating excessive reliance on high-confidence logits. Consequently, the robust model is guided by a target distribution better matched to the robust feature space, while preserving the global cross-modal semantic structure of the original model. We define the distribution consistency calibration module as:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}p_{{rob}}^{i,j}&amp;=\frac{\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta_{{orig}}}(x^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{j})\right)/(\tau/\gamma)\right)}{\sum_{k}\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta_{{orig}}}(x^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{k})\right)/(\tau/\gamma)\right)},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}p_{{rob}}^{i,j}&amp;=\frac{\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta_{{orig}}}(x^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{j})\right)/(\tau/\gamma)\right)}{\sum_{k}\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta_{{orig}}}(x^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{k})\right)/(\tau/\gamma)\right)},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the temperature scaling ratio, and the calibrated temperature is <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is used only to calibrate the pre-trained target distribution, while both calibration and adversarial fine-tuning share <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is tuned to control the distribution sharpness, facilitating robust feature learning during fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Objective Function of AGFT.</span> Following the overall scheme of text-guided adversarial training,
we replace <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{orig}}^{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{orig}}^{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> with the calibrated target distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{rob}}^{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{rob}}^{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the training objective, which is formulated as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L(\bm{x}_{adv},\bm{t},\bm{p}_{{rob}},\tau)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L(\bm{x}_{adv},\bm{t},\bm{p}_{{rob}},\tau)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\mathbb{E}_{i,j}\left[p_{{rob}}^{i,j}\log\frac{\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta}(x_{adv}^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{j})\right)/\tau\right)}{\sum_{k}\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta}(x_{adv}^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{k})\right)/\tau\right)}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\mathbb{E}_{i,j}\left[p_{{rob}}^{i,j}\log\frac{\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta}(x_{adv}^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{j})\right)/\tau\right)}{\sum_{k}\exp\left(\cos\left(f_{\theta}(x_{adv}^{i}),f_{\phi}(t^{k})\right)/\tau\right)}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we define the final alignment-guided adversarial fine-tuning objective function of AGFT as a min-max optimization problem, <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\mathbb{E}_{\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}}\left[\max_{\bm{x}_{adv}\in B(\bm{x},\epsilon)}L(\bm{x}_{adv},\bm{t},\bm{p}_{{rob}},\tau)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\mathbb{E}_{\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}}\left[\max_{\bm{x}_{adv}\in B(\bm{x},\epsilon)}L(\bm{x}_{adv},\bm{t},\bm{p}_{{rob}},\tau)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We adversarially fine-tune CLIP on the ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> training set, following previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. We evaluate both clean and adversarial performance on 15 benchmarks, which include the ImageNet validation set and 14 additional zero-shot transfer datasets across five task families:
generic classification on
Caltech101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>,
Caltech256 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>,
CIFAR10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>,
CIFAR100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>,
and STL10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>;
fine-grained classification on
FGVCAircraft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>,
Flowers102 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>,
Food101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>,
OxfordPets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>,
and StanfordCars <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>;
scene recognition on
SUN397 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>;
texture recognition on
DTD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>;
domain-specialized classification on
EuroSAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>
and PCAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.
For out-of-distribution evaluation, we also consider two variants of ImageNet: ImageNet-R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which features altered textures and local statistics, and ImageNet-S <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, containing sketch-style images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare AGFT with four representative adversarial fine-tuning baselines: TeCoA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, PMG-AFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, TGA-ZSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, and GLADIATOR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. For each method, we report both clean accuracy and robust accuracy across multiple training and evaluation settings, allowing for a comprehensive assessment of AGFT’s effectiveness. Detailed implementation settings for all baselines are provided in the Appendix (<a href="#S6" title="6 Implementation Details of Baselines ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
We adopt the CLIP model with a ViT-B/32 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> image encoder. We fine-tune all parameters of the image encoder while keeping the text encoder frozen. Training is conducted for 10 epochs using SGD with an initial learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, momentum of 0.9, cosine learning-rate decay, and a batch size of 256. During adversarial fine-tuning, adversarial examples are generated using a 2-step PGD with perturbation budgets <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\{1/255,2/255,4/255\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\{1/255,2/255,4/255\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the step size is set to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. For robustness evaluation, we employ a 20-step PGD with a step size of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1/255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1/255</annotation></semantics></math>. The hyperparameters are set as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1/180" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>180</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1/180</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted on two NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Configuration.</span>
Beyond standard PGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, we evaluate robustness using two stronger white-box attacks: the Carlini &amp; Wagner (C&amp;W) attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and AutoAttack (AA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. To assess generalizability against unseen threats, we further include several attack methods: MI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, DI<sup id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, TI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, NI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, PI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and PI-FGSM++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Detailed attack settings are provided in the Appendix (<a href="#S7" title="7 Attack Configurations ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average zero-shot robust accuracy (%) under PGD-20 attacks with varying perturbation budgets during <span id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">inference</span>.
</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.45pt;padding-bottom:-0.45pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T3.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Method</span></th>
