</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-tuning with Larger Perturbation Budgets.</span>
To evaluate different defense regimes, we also adversarially fine-tune models with larger perturbation budgets. The results in <a href="#S4.T4" title="In 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show that AGFT consistently outperforms all evaluated baselines in both clean and robust accuracy across various training radii. In particular, under <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=4/255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=4/255</annotation></semantics></math>, AGFT improves clean accuracy by 7.7% and robust accuracy by 3.8% over the best baseline. Such consistent performance confirms that AGFT remains highly effective even under stronger adversarial training settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness against Stronger Attacks.</span>
In addition to the standard PGD-20 attack, we further evaluate adversarial robustness under two stronger white-box attacks: the Carlini &amp; Wagner (C&amp;W) attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and AutoAttack (AA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. AGFT consistently achieves the best robust accuracy under both attacks across various perturbation budgets. Such broad generalization confirms that the improvements of AGFT are not limited to PGD evaluations and generalize well to stronger attack scenarios. Detailed per-dataset results are provided in the Appendix (<a href="#S9" title="9 Additional Results ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness against Diverse Unseen Attacks.</span>
To further evaluate robustness generalization beyond the specific attack configuration used during training, we consider a diverse set of attack methods, including MI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, DI<sup id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, TI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, NI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, PI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and PI-FGSM++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In addition to untargeted attacks, we also evaluate targeted attacks with two target selection strategies: random target selection and least-likely target selection. As shown in <a href="#S4.T6" title="In 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, AGFT achieves the best average robust accuracy across all 15 attack settings, outperforming the strongest baseline by 3.6%. These results confirm that AGFT provides stronger robustness generalization across diverse unseen attack types.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.T6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Zero-shot robust accuracy (%) under diverse <span id="S4.T6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unseen untargeted and targeted attacks</span>
with the perturbation budget <math id="S4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1/255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1/255</annotation></semantics></math>.
Targeted attacks with random target selection are denoted by
ATT<sub id="S4.T6.7.2" class="ltx_sub">T</sub>, and those with least-likely target selection are denoted by
ATT<sub id="S4.T6.7.3" class="ltx_sub">TL</sub>.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T6.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:42pt;vertical-align:-19.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-113.5pt,9.6pt) scale(0.686332246650181,0.686332246650181) ;">
<table id="S4.T6.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T6.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.5pt;padding-bottom:-0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T6.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
