<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering">
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:144%;">Supplementary Material 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Implementation Details of Baselines</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we briefly describe the baseline methods and summarize their implementation details.</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TeCoA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> introduces a text-guided contrastive adversarial training objective and improves adversarial robustness by fine-tuning the CLIP image encoder. We follow the official implementation and hyperparameter settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PMG-AFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> extends TeCoA by leveraging supervision from the pre-trained model to alleviate overfitting. Because the original method was developed on a smaller-scale dataset rather than ImageNet, its default hyperparameters may not be optimal in our setting. We therefore tune only the learning rate and report the best-performing configuration in <a href="#S7.T11" title="In 7 Attack Configurations ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TGA-ZSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> enhances adversarial robustness through an attention refinement module and an attention-based model constraint module. Similar to PMG-AFT, we tune the learning rate to obtain a stronger configuration for our setting, as reported in <a href="#S7.T11" title="In 7 Attack Configurations ‣ AGFT: Alignment-Guided Fine-Tuning for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GLADIATOR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> improves robustness by maximizing the effective rank of features and applying feature noise modulation. As the official code is not currently accessible, we adopt the performance reported by the authors in their publication.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Attack Configurations</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to PGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, we evaluate eight additional attacks, which fall into four categories:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strong Attacks</span>:
Carlini &amp; Wagner (C&amp;W) attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and AutoAttack (AA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimization-based Attacks</span>:
MI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and NI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data-augmentation-based Attacks</span>:
DI<sup id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and TI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patch-wise Attacks</span>:
PI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and PI-FGSM++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To adapt the C&amp;W attack to the <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm threat model,
we optimize the objective within a PGD framework, using a fixed step size and projecting the perturbation onto the feasible <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> ball at each iteration. For AutoAttack, we use its two APGD variants, namely APGD-CE and APGD-DLR.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to standard untargeted attacks, we also consider targeted attacks, where adversarial examples are optimized to be classified into a specified target class.
The target class is either selected uniformly at random or chosen as the least-likely class under the clean-image prediction.
We evaluate targeted versions of PGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, MI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, NI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, DI<sup id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, and TI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 11</span>: </span><span id="S7.T11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average accuracy (%) under different hyperparameters for PMG-AFT and TGA-ZSR.</span></figcaption>
